# Companies in YOUR tracker whose names may legitimately appear in the public tree, one
# per line, matched case-insensitively and whole-word by tools/make-public-tree.
#
# Copy this file to `.tracked-company-exceptions` (gitignored) and list your own.
#
# WHAT THE CHECK DOES. Every other content check reads a list someone remembered to
# write. This one derives its terms from your tracker database instead — every company
# name in it — and fails if one turns up in the tree about to be published. A roadmap,
# a changelog or a test fixture that names a company you are applying to is exactly the
# leak `.private-terms` exists to stop, except nobody can maintain a denylist of fifty
# employers that grows every week. Deriving it means the list is never out of date.
#
# WHY THERE HAS TO BE AN ESCAPE HATCH. A tracked company can also be a vendor, a
# standard, a protocol or the maker of a tool this project legitimately depends on. The
# check cannot tell "I am interviewing there" from "we call their API", so it asks you
# once per name and then stops asking.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS GITIGNORED, like `.private-terms`. It is a list of companies you
# track. That it records them as *allowed* does not make it less disclosing: the set
# itself is your job search. The mechanism ships; the names do not.
#
# Add a name here only when you have read the reported line and it is genuinely about
# the technology, not about your search. When in doubt, reword the line instead — an
# exemption is permanent and silent, and an exemption no file needs is a blind spot
# waiting for the next file that matches.

# an-llm-vendor-whose-api-this-uses
# a-standards-body-referenced-in-docs
